---
/**
 * ReportRoutes — the body of /report/, the entry-anchored correction page.
 *
 * WHY A PAGE RATHER THAN A LINK STRAIGHT TO GITHUB
 * ------------------------------------------------
 * Sending a card's report link directly to a prefilled GitHub issue is one hop shorter,
 * and for a reader with an account it is strictly better. But a GitHub account is a real
 * exclusion for this audience — a bench scientist in cell ag is meaningfully less likely
 * to hold one than a software reader — and that reader meets a sign-in wall with the
 * entry id stranded behind it. This page is where the id is shown plainly and every route
 * carries it, so no reader is turned away holding a correction they cannot deliver.
 *
 * WHY THE ID IS RESOLVED IN THE BROWSER
 * -------------------------------------
 * The site is static files on GitHub Pages, so `?item=` can only be read client-side.
 * Three consequences the markup below is built around:
 *
 *   1. The query string is attacker-controlled. It is validated against `isItemId` and
 *      then assigned via `textContent` / `href` — never `innerHTML` — so a hostile value
 *      cannot become markup even if the grammar were later loosened.
 *   2. With JavaScript off, nothing resolves. So the page renders the UNANCHORED routes
 *      server-side and the script upgrades them; a no-JS reader gets a working, honest
 *      page rather than an empty one.
 *   3. Cloudflare Web Analytics discards query strings, so page views here are countable
 *      in total but not per entry. The per-entry signal comes from the existing
 *      outbound-click event, which keeps the destination URL.
 */
import { COMMUNITY_PATH } from '../lib/community';
import { CORRECTION_EMAIL, correctionIssueUrl, correctionMailto } from '../lib/report';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
// Unanchored fallbacks. The script replaces both when a valid `?item=` is present.
const issueUrl = correctionIssueUrl(null);
const mailto = correctionMailto(null);
---

<div class="rp">
  {/* Identified-entry banner. Hidden until the script validates an id, so a reader who
      lands here with no query string, or with a malformed one, never sees an empty slot. */}
  <p class="rp-id" id="caail-report-id" hidden>
    <span class="rp-id-label">This report is about entry</span>
    <code class="rp-id-value" id="caail-report-id-value"></code>
    <button class="rp-copy" id="caail-report-copy" type="button" hidden>Copy</button>
    {/* Announces the copy result. Present from first render so the announcement is not
        the insertion itself, which some screen readers drop. */}
    <span class="rp-copy-status" id="caail-report-copy-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></span>
  </p>

  {/* Reached with no id from two quite different places: someone who navigated here
      directly, and someone following the homepage topics band, whose complaint is about a
      whole subject theme and has no single entry behind it. So this says what the id buys
      and where to find one, rather than reading as a step the reader got wrong. */}
  <p class="rp-none" id="caail-report-none">
    Every route below works as it is. If your report is about one entry, though, it is much
    quicker to act on when it names that entry: entry cards on the Papers, Software,
    Databases and Datasets pages carry their own
    <span class="rp-nowrap">Report an issue</span> link, which brings the id here with it.
    Reports about a whole subject theme, or about the library as a whole, need no id.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-to-report">How to report it</h2>

  <p>
    Pick whichever route you already have an account for. All three reach the same person,
    and all three carry the entry id.
  </p>

  <ul class="rp-routes">
    <li>
      <a id="caail-report-github" href={issueUrl}>Open a correction on GitHub</a>
      <span class="rp-note">
        Needs a GitHub account. The form is prefilled with the entry id, and the report,
        the discussion and the eventual fix all end up in one public place with a permanent
        link.
      </span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a id="caail-report-email" href={mailto}>Send an email</a>
      <span class="rp-note">
        No account needed. Goes to <code>{CORRECTION_EMAIL}</code>, with the entry id already
        in the subject line.
      </span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href={`${base}${COMMUNITY_PATH}`}>Say so in Slack</a>
      <span class="rp-note">
        No GitHub account needed. Paste the entry id into your message so a curator knows
        which entry you mean.
      </span>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-is-useful">What is useful to say</h2>

  <p>
    What is wrong, what it should say instead, and where you are reading that from. The last
    of those is the one that saves the most time: a curator has to find the source before
    changing anything, so a pointer to the paper's methods section, the dataset's own
    documentation, or the repository's licence file turns a report into a fix.
  </p>

  <p>
    You do not need to be sure. Reports are welcome whether or not they hold up, and an entry
    that survives being challenged is worth as much to us as one that gets corrected.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-happens-next">What happens next</h2>

  <p>
    A curator checks the report against the source and, where it holds up, corrects the entry
    through the library's authoring backend. We do not promise a turnaround time: the review
    path runs through one person, and a schedule we cannot keep is worse than none.
  </p>

  <p>
    CAAIL's curation is openly in progress. It indexes a curated subset rather than a census
    and has not measured its own recall, so a reader who knows a subfield well is often the
    first to notice a specific entry is wrong. That is the signal this page exists to catch.
  </p>
</div>

<script>
  import { isItemId, correctionIssueUrl, correctionMailto } from '../lib/report';

  const raw = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get('item');
  const id = isItemId(raw) ? raw : null;

  const banner = document.getElementById('caail-report-id');
  const value = document.getElementById('caail-report-id-value');
  const none = document.getElementById('caail-report-none');
  const gh = document.getElementById('caail-report-github') as HTMLAnchorElement | null;
  const mail = document.getElementById('caail-report-email') as HTMLAnchorElement | null;
  const copy = document.getElementById('caail-report-copy') as HTMLButtonElement | null;
  const status = document.getElementById('caail-report-copy-status');

  if (id) {
    // textContent, never innerHTML: `id` has passed the grammar check, and this keeps it
    // inert regardless of what that grammar is later widened to admit.
    if (value) value.textContent = id;
    if (banner) banner.hidden = false;
    if (none) none.hidden = true;
    if (gh) gh.href = correctionIssueUrl(id);
    if (mail) mail.href = correctionMailto(id);

    // The copy button is pure enhancement — the id is selectable text without it — so it
    // stays hidden where the Clipboard API is unavailable rather than failing on click.
    if (copy && navigator.clipboard) {
      copy.hidden = false;
      let clear: number | undefined;
      // Re-announcing needs the region to CHANGE, and a second copy writes the same
      // string as the first, so without clearing it a screen-reader user hears nothing
      // the second time. src/lib/tabs.ts solves it the same way, and
      // homepage-agent.spec.ts has a regression test named for exactly this.
      const announce = (msg: string) => {
        if (!status) return;
        if (clear !== undefined) window.clearTimeout(clear);
        status.textContent = msg;
        clear = window.setTimeout(() => {
          status.textContent = '';
          clear = undefined;
        }, 2000);
      };
      copy.addEventListener('click', async () => {
        try {
          await navigator.clipboard.writeText(id);
          announce('Entry id copied.');
        } catch {
          announce('Could not copy. Select the id and copy it manually.');
        }
      });
    }
  }
</script>

<style>
  /* `hidden` is how this page switches between its two states, and a class that sets
     `display` silently defeats it: a class selector outranks the user-agent's
     `[hidden] { display: none }`, so `.rp-id`'s flex below would render the banner even
     while the attribute is set. Restated here at higher specificity than any rule in
     this file so every hideable element in the page obeys the attribute. */
  .rp [hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  .rp-id {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0.6rem 0.8rem;
    background: var(--caail-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--caail-border);
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
  }
  .rp-id-label {
    color: var(--caail-muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  .rp-id-value {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .rp-copy {
    font: inherit;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    padding: 0.15rem 0.6rem;
    min-height: 1.5rem;
    color: var(--caail-muted);
    background: var(--caail-bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--caail-border);
    border-radius: 0.35rem;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .rp-copy:hover,
  .rp-copy:focus-visible {
    color: var(--caail-link);
    border-color: var(--caail-link);
  }
  .rp-copy-status {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--caail-muted);
  }
  .rp-nowrap {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .rp-routes > li {
    margin-block: 0.75rem;
  }
  .rp-note {
    display: block;
    color: var(--caail-muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
